.TH CTIME 3
.SH NAME
ctime, localtime, gmtime, asctime, tzset \- convert date and time to ASCII
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <time.h>

char *
ctime(clock)
long *clock;

struct tm *
localtime(clock)
long *clock;

struct tm *
gmtime(clock)
long *clock;

char *
asctime(tm)
struct tm *tm;

tzset()

extern long timezone;
extern int daylight;
extern char *tzname[2];
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A time, as
.BR time (2)
returns it, is a long: the number of seconds since 00:00:00 Greenwich
Mean Time, January 1, 1970. These functions take its 32 bits as an
unsigned number, which lasts until 06:28:15 GMT on February 7, 2106;
nothing on XENIX is dated before 1970, to need negative times.
.PP
.B localtime
and
.B gmtime
take a pointer to such a time and return a pointer to a structure with
its parts, in local time and in Greenwich Mean Time:
.PP
.nf
	struct tm {
		int tm_sec;	/* seconds (0 \- 59) */
		int tm_min;	/* minutes (0 \- 59) */
		int tm_hour;	/* hours (0 \- 23) */
		int tm_mday;	/* day of month (1 \- 31) */
		int tm_mon;	/* month of year (0 \- 11) */
		int tm_year;	/* year \- 1900 */
		int tm_wday;	/* day of week (Sunday = 0) */
		int tm_yday;	/* day of year (0 \- 365) */
		int tm_isdst;	/* nonzero in daylight saving time */
	};
.fi
.PP
.B asctime
converts such a structure to a string of 26 characters, in this form,
newline and NUL included:
.PP
.nf
	Sun Sep 16 01:03:52 1973\en\e0
.fi
.PP
.B ctime(clock)
is
.BR asctime(localtime(clock)) .
.PP
The local time zone is given by the environment variable TZ, as three
letters naming the standard time zone, the number of hours it is west
of Greenwich (negative to the east), and three letters naming the
daylight saving time zone if there is one: TZ=EST5EDT, or TZ=PST8PDT,
or TZ=GMT0. When TZ is not set, or is empty, the zone is XENIX's own
default, PST8PDT.
.B tzset
reads TZ and sets the external variables:
.I timezone
to the difference between GMT and local standard time in seconds,
.I daylight
to nonzero if a daylight saving zone is named, and
.I tzname
to the two zones' names.
localtime calls tzset the first time it is called.
.PP
Daylight saving time is applied in the United States manner, by the
rules of the year: from 2 a.m. on the last Sunday in April to 2 a.m. on
the last Sunday in October, but from January 6 in 1974 and from
February 23 in 1975. From 1987 on, as the law of 1986 provides, it
begins on the first Sunday in April instead; and from 2007 on it runs
from the second Sunday in March to the first Sunday in November.
.SH SEE ALSO
time(2), getenv(3), environ(5)
.SH HISTORY
A
.B ctime
function first appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX, and
.BR asctime ,
.B gmtime
and
.B localtime
in Version 5.
.B tzset
and the TZ variable are those of System V.
.SH BUGS
The results are in static areas, which the next call overwrites.
.PP
Only the United States rules for daylight saving time are known.
